1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. Nb5 Qd8 8.
c3 a6 9. Na3 c5 10. Nf3 Nc6 11. Nc2 c4 12. g3 O-O 13. Bh3 Qe7 14. Nh4 $6 f6 15.
f4 $2 fxe5 16. dxe5 Ndxe5 $5 ({This works out well and} 16... Rxf4 $1 {is also
strong. White's play to here has been pretty terrible and continues to be so
for a few more moves.}) 17. fxe5 Nxe5 18. Rf1 Nd3+ 19. Ke2 Bd7 (19... e5 $17)
20. Qd2 e5 21. Bxd7 Qxd7 22. Ne3 Qh3 23. Nhf5 Rad8 24. Kd1 d4 {White is being
smashed off the board and deservedly so. But he now gets lucky.} 25. Qg2 dxe3
$4 {[#]} ({Black should simply settle for a mundane win after} 25... Qxg2 26.
Nxg2 g6 $19 {Instead he goes after a flashy finish involving what he thinks is
a temporary queen sacrifice, but it rebounds mightily...}) 26. Ne7+ $1 Kh8 27.
Ng6+ $1 Kg8 28. Qd5+ $1 {White's triple whammy is very attractive but I feel
we have to label it a cheapo because of the poor play that preceded it.
Sources: Birmingham Daily Post, 3 January 1901; Pall Mall Gazette, 16 January
1901; Dover Express - Friday 18 January 1901; (the finish) 'The Brilliant
Touch' by Walter Korn (Pitman, 1950), p13} 1-0

